{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 32, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink petals dusting the streets like confetti. The air smelled of incense and rain-wet stone. I spent mornings wandering Kinkaku-ji, its golden pavilion shimmering above a still pond, and afternoons sipping matcha in tiny teahouses where silence felt sacred.\n\nTokyo was a different rhythm—neon pulses in Shibuya, the hum of bullet trains, and vending machines that sold everything from hot coffee to umbrellas. I ate ramen at a 20-seat stall in Shinjuku, where the chef nodded without words and served me broth so rich it warmed my bones. In Akihabara, I marveled at robot cafés and pixelated anime billboards, but found myself most drawn to the quiet corner of a used bookstore, flipping through vintage manga under soft lamps.\n\nOne evening, I took the train to Hakone. As dusk fell, Mount Fuji appeared through mist—majestic, silent, crowned with snow. I soaked in an onsen under a sky stitched with stars, the hot water easing days of walking. No one spoke. Only the hiss of steam and the distant chime of a temple bell.\n\nI left with a small wooden fox charm from Fushimi Inari—thousands of vermilion torii gates winding up the mountain like a fiery dragon. I didn’t buy souvenirs, but I brought back stillness. Japan doesn’t shout. It whispers: in the rustle of a kimono, the precision of a folded napkin, the pause before a bow.\n\nI didn’t just visit Japan. I listened to it.\n\nAnd it changed me.\n\n(Word count: 298)", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.09506416320800781}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.31332796812057495}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.002152984729036689}, {"token": "3", "logprob": -0.0005376085755415261}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -2.3841855